From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:38:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] doc/examples/filter_audio: generate a continuous sine wave

The phase of the generated tone was computed from frame_num + j. As one
period is exactly FRAME_SIZE samples long, the sample following the
last one of frame n (phase n + FRAME_SIZE - 1) has phase n, but the
next frame starts at phase n + 1, so one sample of the wave was skipped
at every frame boundary.

Every frame holds a whole number of periods, so the phase only depends
on the position within the frame.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FZ4G6798n6Ua4qu6xwWu3S
---
 doc/examples/filter_audio.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/doc/examples/filter_audio.c b/doc/examples/filter_audio.c
index 824fd83..760c605 100644
--- a/doc/examples/filter_audio.c
+++ b/doc/examples/filter_audio.c
@@ -258,7 +258,7 @@ static int get_input(AVFrame *frame, int frame_num)
         float *data = (float*)frame->extended_data[i];
 
         for (j = 0; j < frame->nb_samples; j++)
-            data[j] = sin(2 * M_PI * (frame_num + j) * (i + 1) / FRAME_SIZE);
+            data[j] = sin(2 * M_PI * j * (i + 1) / FRAME_SIZE);
     }
 
     return 0;
